This is Prince Harry calling himself Harry Diversity or something, renaming himself.

116
00:07:14,860 --> 00:07:15,900
And actually…

117
00:07:15,900 --> 00:07:16,820
No, Harry Equity.

118
00:07:17,440 --> 00:07:18,880
Harry Equity, yeah.

119
00:07:18,960 --> 00:07:19,940
While living in his palace.

120
00:07:20,340 --> 00:07:21,340
Yeah, that's what he'd call himself.

121
00:07:21,620 --> 00:07:22,300
Yeah, Harry Equity.

122
00:07:22,460 --> 00:07:27,340
Anyway, so everybody goes to the Tuileries on the 20th of September to register.

123
00:07:27,860 --> 00:07:34,200
And they do so in an atmosphere of apocalyptic dread because they know that 140 miles away,

124
00:07:34,200 --> 00:07:40,380
the Prussians are there and that their troops, their France's troops, have basically caught up with the Prussians.

125
00:07:40,620 --> 00:07:46,800
And the Duke of Brunswick, just to remind people, has said that he is going to inflict a biblical fate on Paris.

126
00:07:47,120 --> 00:07:47,860
He has, yeah.

127
00:07:47,980 --> 00:07:51,700
A vengeance which will be forever remembered, or words to that effect.

128
00:07:51,860 --> 00:07:52,280
So, yes.

129
00:07:52,520 --> 00:07:57,540
Now, of these guys who've turned up to register, about half of them are lawyers.

130
00:07:57,680 --> 00:07:58,440
So if you're…

131
00:07:58,440 --> 00:08:02,220
We've got a lot of lawyers who are members of Arrested History Club, so they will love all this.

132
00:08:02,320 --> 00:08:03,640
They will say this is a very good sign.

133
00:08:04,200 --> 00:08:08,180
There's also loads of doctors, loads of civil servants, loads of actors, journalists.

134
00:08:09,360 --> 00:08:11,000
They're very young, by and large.

135
00:08:11,080 --> 00:08:12,600
About half of them are under 40.

136
00:08:12,660 --> 00:08:14,280
About a quarter of them are under 35.

137
00:08:14,900 --> 00:08:19,600
The younger they are, the more committed and more militant they are, by and large.

138
00:08:20,380 --> 00:08:21,620
So, you know, these aren't…

139
00:08:22,080 --> 00:08:27,660
A lot of them have got experience in kind of local governments and stuff, but these aren't incredibly seasoned people.

140
00:08:27,840 --> 00:08:32,020
They are excited and excitable, I think it's fair to say.

141
00:08:32,180 --> 00:08:33,900
Kind of quite a student union vibe.

142
00:08:34,200 --> 00:08:34,500
Definitely.

143
00:08:34,600 --> 00:08:36,700
I mean, this is the birth of the student union, really, isn't it?

144
00:08:36,720 --> 00:08:37,660
I think it probably is, yeah.

145
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:39,560
Motions, all that kind of stuff.

146
00:08:39,780 --> 00:08:40,520
Yeah, they love it.

147
00:08:40,620 --> 00:08:45,780
And they're there all night, you know, just endlessly arguing and showing off in that student union.

148
00:08:46,040 --> 00:08:46,480
And drinking.

149
00:08:47,060 --> 00:08:47,500
Exactly.

150
00:08:48,100 --> 00:08:54,200
Next day, 21st of September, they assemble at the riding school, the Manège, for their first proper session.

151
00:08:54,380 --> 00:09:00,620
And this is the bit that you started with, Jérôme Pétion, because they've come straight to the central question.

152
00:09:00,880 --> 00:09:02,020
Let's reboot France.

153
00:09:02,900 --> 00:09:04,180
And it must be a…

154
00:09:05,100 --> 00:09:08,200
There's an actor, of course, called Collot de Bois.

155
00:09:08,300 --> 00:09:10,580
He ends up on the Committee of Public Safety later on.

156
00:09:10,920 --> 00:09:14,000
He says, let's just abolish the monarchy right away.

157
00:09:14,120 --> 00:09:15,720
No need for referendums and all that stuff.

158
00:09:15,820 --> 00:09:16,560
Let's just go for it.

159
00:09:17,200 --> 00:09:18,840
Some of the deputies say, really?

160
00:09:18,900 --> 00:09:22,820
No, no, you wouldn't let the people have a say on such a massive question?

161
00:09:23,520 --> 00:09:25,120
And then the Bishop of Blois.

162
00:09:25,740 --> 00:09:30,580
So this is an interesting thing, because again, it complicates the slightly sort of stereotypical sense of the French Revolution.

163
00:09:30,640 --> 00:09:33,560
Because it's a bishop, Henri Grégoire, who says,

164
00:09:34,200 --> 00:09:37,420
Kings are in the moral world what monsters are in the physical world.

165
00:09:37,600 --> 00:09:41,220
Their courts are the workshops of crime and the lairs of tyrants.

166
00:09:41,520 --> 00:09:43,920
In other words, no referendum.

167
00:09:44,140 --> 00:09:44,880
Let's just get rid.

168
00:09:45,140 --> 00:09:46,720
And they vote.

169
00:09:46,920 --> 00:09:51,160
And then, of course, as so often happens in these kinds of scenarios, very student union.

170
00:09:51,800 --> 00:09:54,600
Once people work out which way the wind is blowing…

171
00:09:54,600 --> 00:09:55,100
They all pile in.

172
00:09:55,200 --> 00:09:56,060
Yeah, they all pile in.

173
00:09:56,100 --> 00:09:57,380
So they make the vote unanimous.

174
00:09:57,520 --> 00:09:59,440
They all start shouting, vive la nation.

175
00:09:59,920 --> 00:10:01,100
They're all terribly excited.

176
00:10:01,560 --> 00:10:03,540
The world has started again.

177
00:10:03,540 --> 00:10:13,360
But Dominic, just to pick up one, one thing that is intriguing, and it's a kind of absence is that there isn't a kind of formal proclamation of the Republic.

178
00:10:13,840 --> 00:10:16,820
There's no official decree kind of installing it.

179
00:10:16,940 --> 00:10:23,360
And I just mentioned that because in the next episode, when we come to the trial of the King, I think that is significant.

180
00:10:23,580 --> 00:10:33,060
And I think that that is something that frames what will happen with the trial and the execution of the King, because although it is a new beginning, although they've had the vote.

181
00:10:33,420 --> 00:10:38,200
It's kind of, they know what they're getting rid of, but they don't yet know what they're establishing.

182
00:10:38,580 --> 00:10:46,020
Yeah, I think that's a very fair point, actually, that there is a sense of, there's a sense of historical time beginning again, but they don't really know what they all want.

183
00:10:46,080 --> 00:10:49,740
Because I suppose, Tom, they don't all want the same thing as we shall see in this episode.

184
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:55,400
So if you enjoy political factionalism, this is definitely the rest is history episode for you.

185
00:10:55,980 --> 00:11:00,340
Anyway, one of the interesting things about the French Revolution that we've talked about so much is the way it faces two ways.

186
00:11:00,420 --> 00:11:02,300
On the one hand, they're looking back to the Roman Republic.

187
00:11:02,380 --> 00:11:03,040
As you have done.

188
00:11:03,060 --> 00:11:03,960
Described so many times.

189
00:11:04,200 --> 00:11:11,840
And on the other hand, they're genuinely thinking this is not just a whole new chapter, but it's basically a new, a new volume in world history.

190
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:21,940
So it's, they say from September the 20th, all state documents must bear the date year one of the French Republic.

191
00:11:22,340 --> 00:11:30,920
So this is where we get the beginning of the sort of French revolutionary calendar, the numbering system year one, year two, and so on and so forth.

192
00:11:31,340 --> 00:11:32,880
But of course, whether they actually get.

193
00:11:32,880 --> 00:11:35,160
Beyond year one is not in their hands.

194
00:11:35,260 --> 00:11:36,860
It depends on events out on the battlefield.

195
00:11:37,260 --> 00:11:42,800
So let us now, let us leave Paris, turn the camera and turn the camera.

196
00:11:42,920 --> 00:11:43,220
Exactly.

197
00:11:43,480 --> 00:11:44,800
You know how my mind works.

198
00:11:46,420 --> 00:11:52,100
So remember where we got to the Prussians have been marching all the way west from the border.

199
00:11:52,420 --> 00:11:58,540
They've got to the, the Argonne forest that's 130 or so miles away from Paris.

200
00:11:58,720 --> 00:11:59,900
It's pouring with rain.

201
00:12:00,020 --> 00:12:01,480
Very Agincourt scenes, Tom.

202
00:12:01,580 --> 00:12:02,540
There's only one man.

203
00:12:02,540 --> 00:12:03,320
There's only one man who can stop them.

204
