And so the radicals of one month can suddenly find themselves the reactionaries of the next

444
00:27:03,320 --> 00:27:03,680
month.

445
00:27:03,860 --> 00:27:07,020
And it means that there's an impetus always to go further and further.

446
00:27:07,180 --> 00:27:10,980
Whether you were backing, you know, the people are always right, or whether you were saying

447
00:27:10,980 --> 00:27:12,920
virtue is manifest in violence.

448
00:27:13,240 --> 00:27:18,200
If you start saying, oh, I'm not sure about that, then immediately you are a reactionary.

449
00:27:18,280 --> 00:27:18,560
Absolutely.

450
00:27:18,560 --> 00:27:21,360
And that is a trend that you see again and again and again.

451
00:27:21,440 --> 00:27:22,920
I mean, throughout the history of 19th century.

452
00:27:23,740 --> 00:27:24,100
Yeah.

453
00:27:24,200 --> 00:27:26,160
And right, you know, right the way up to the present day.

454
00:27:26,260 --> 00:27:26,540
Absolutely.

455
00:27:26,800 --> 00:27:27,380
You're totally right.

456
00:27:27,480 --> 00:27:28,420
That sort of ratchet effect.

457
00:27:28,700 --> 00:27:32,140
And in fact, I was just thinking if somebody missed a couple of episodes of the series,

458
00:27:32,500 --> 00:27:36,640
you know, if they'd skipped ahead, they'd be like, hold on, those people were on the

459
00:27:36,640 --> 00:27:38,240
far left two episodes ago.

460
00:27:38,300 --> 00:27:41,980
Now you're describing them as, you know, counter-revolutionary reactionaries.

461
00:27:42,040 --> 00:27:45,620
Because as you say, the center of gravity is always moving leftwards.

462
00:27:45,920 --> 00:27:49,620
What's on the Girondins' mind as well, by the way, is the fact that during the September

463
00:27:50,100 --> 00:27:50,460
massacres.

464
00:27:50,700 --> 00:27:51,060
Yeah.

465
00:27:51,060 --> 00:27:55,920
There is some evidence that Robespierre and Marat had actually toyed with the idea

466
00:27:55,920 --> 00:27:57,260
of killing them too.

467
00:27:57,780 --> 00:28:02,940
So Robespierre, on the day the massacres had broken out, the 2nd of September, he had been

468
00:28:02,940 --> 00:28:08,600
giving a speech in the commune, the sort of city council of Paris, in which he had said

469
00:28:08,600 --> 00:28:14,360
that the Girondins were secret agents of the Prussians and perfidious intriguers working

470
00:28:14,360 --> 00:28:15,320
against French liberty.

471
00:28:15,640 --> 00:28:19,600
Now anyone who's listened to the whole of this series will know how bonkers that is,

472
00:28:19,600 --> 00:28:23,440
because the Girondins were the key people in getting France into the war in the first

473
00:28:23,440 --> 00:28:23,740
place.

474
00:28:24,640 --> 00:28:29,000
But of course, at the time, everybody believes in this idea of the mask of patriotism, that

475
00:28:29,000 --> 00:28:33,080
the more patriotic you appear to be, the more likely it is that you are in fact a traitor.

476
00:28:33,700 --> 00:28:38,540
So the commune had issued a warrant for some of the Girondins' arrest, which was never

477
00:28:38,540 --> 00:28:39,180
carried out.

478
00:28:40,560 --> 00:28:47,660
But Brissot and Roland and these people, they know about this and they say, my God, Robespierre

479
00:28:47,660 --> 00:28:48,400
wants to kill us.

480
00:28:48,400 --> 00:28:51,500
He wanted to kill us and it's just luck that he didn't.

481
00:28:51,820 --> 00:28:57,740
And so they become convinced, well they are now convinced, that basically it's kill or

482
00:28:57,740 --> 00:28:58,120
be killed.

483
00:28:58,620 --> 00:29:03,860
So in that sense, right from the moment the convention meets, which is 21st of September,

484
00:29:04,580 --> 00:29:08,620
it's not like a normal parliament that we would know today because there is no sense

485
00:29:08,620 --> 00:29:10,480
of pluralism.

486
00:29:10,700 --> 00:29:13,980
There's no sense that they're going to be having discussions and you win some, you lose

487
00:29:13,980 --> 00:29:14,940
some, all that kind of thing.

488
00:29:15,140 --> 00:29:16,260
No sense of a loyal opposition?

489
00:29:17,140 --> 00:29:17,580
None.

490
00:29:17,580 --> 00:29:21,120
So by definition, this is about virtue versus corruption.

491
00:29:21,540 --> 00:29:24,040
It's about the Republic versus counter-revolution.

492
00:29:24,600 --> 00:29:27,080
To be a dissenter is to be a traitor.

493
00:29:27,660 --> 00:29:31,800
And so I think from the very first moment they take their seats, it is obvious there

494
00:29:31,800 --> 00:29:38,440
is absolutely going to be a showdown and that whoever loses that will probably end up on

495
00:29:38,440 --> 00:29:38,920
the guillotine.

496
00:29:39,360 --> 00:29:45,660
And if you had to put your money on somebody at this point, September 1792, you would probably

497
00:29:45,660 --> 00:29:46,580
put it on the Girondins.

498
00:29:46,580 --> 00:29:51,300
They have a bit more of a majority, they have a bit more self-confidence, they control

499
00:29:51,300 --> 00:29:53,540
the presidency of the convention.

500
00:29:54,380 --> 00:29:58,820
And so right from the beginning with the September massacre so fresh in their minds, they say,

501
00:29:58,900 --> 00:30:01,560
right, let's settle this once and for all, let's do this.

502
00:30:01,800 --> 00:30:05,100
And right away they go for the Montagnards' throats.

503
00:30:06,020 --> 00:30:06,380
Wow.

504
00:30:06,440 --> 00:30:10,560
Well, I think that is too much excitement for one half of an episode.

505
00:30:10,660 --> 00:30:14,180
So I think we should take a break here and when we come back, we will see what the upshot

506
00:30:14,180 --> 00:30:16,560
of this Girondin attempt to win.

507
00:30:16,580 --> 00:30:18,900
To take down the Montagnards actually is.

508
00:30:24,020 --> 00:30:24,960
Hello, welcome back.

509
00:30:25,720 --> 00:30:28,380
We left you at a moment of high drama.

510
00:30:29,220 --> 00:30:32,980
The Girondins are about to take on the Montagnards in the national convention.

511
00:30:33,320 --> 00:30:34,980
Dominic put people out of their misery.

512
00:30:35,080 --> 00:30:35,520
What happens?

513
00:30:35,820 --> 00:30:40,660
So they make their move, Tom, two days after the Battle of Valmy, 22nd of September.

514
00:30:40,780 --> 00:30:43,300
The convention has only really been up and running for a day and a bit.

515
00:30:44,600 --> 00:30:49,040
And a dozen speakers, one after the other, Girondins get up and lay into the Montagnards.

516
00:30:49,160 --> 00:30:49,640
And they won't.

517
00:30:49,640 --> 00:30:53,780
Their plan is to convince the convention the Montagnards are traitors and they should

518
00:30:53,780 --> 00:30:56,220
be arrested and thrown out of the convention.

519
00:30:56,740 --> 00:31:01,440
They say they are anarchists, they are murderers, they are levelers, interestingly.

520
00:31:01,740 --> 00:31:02,040
Oh, right.

521
00:31:02,220 --> 00:31:05,260
A word that will be very familiar to people who know about 17th century England.

522
00:31:05,700 --> 00:31:09,440
But don't they also compare them to Caesar, Crassus, and Pompey?

523
00:31:09,440 --> 00:31:09,940
They do.

524
00:31:10,020 --> 00:31:11,240
The first round version of ancient Rome.

525
00:31:11,300 --> 00:31:11,360
They do.

526
00:31:11,480 --> 00:31:14,860
They're very promiscuous with their historical analogies, I think it's fair to say.

527
00:31:14,900 --> 00:31:17,940
Because Brissot says, on the one hand, he says they're the hydra of anarchy.

528
00:31:18,060 --> 00:31:19,080
They want to level everything.

529
00:31:19,640 --> 00:31:24,480
And on the other hand, he says Robespierre, Marat, and Danton are the new Crassus, Caesar,

530
00:31:24,560 --> 00:31:25,660
and Pompey, exactly as you said.

531
00:31:26,500 --> 00:31:29,540
And Robespierre and Danton say, oh, this is absolute nonsense, you know, I don't even

532
00:31:29,540 --> 00:31:29,960
like him.

533
00:31:30,060 --> 00:31:31,220
I have no...

534
00:31:31,580 --> 00:31:33,340
We're not a triumvirate at all.

535
00:31:34,100 --> 00:31:35,520
Marat, he obviously is...

536
00:31:36,000 --> 00:31:38,980
He's making his debut, effectively, in front-line politics.

537
00:31:39,000 --> 00:31:39,240
Wow.

538
00:31:39,240 --> 00:31:40,400
What a way to do it.

539
00:31:40,620 --> 00:31:44,780
And he says, I'm not a traitor, but if you're accusing me of trying to set up a dictatorship,

540
00:31:44,940 --> 00:31:45,700
well, I'll be frank with you.

541
00:31:45,740 --> 00:31:46,840
I think a dictatorship would be brilliant.

542
00:31:46,900 --> 00:31:47,560
I'd love a dictatorship.

543
00:31:48,620 --> 00:31:49,560
And he's then saying...

544
00:31:49,560 --> 00:31:49,720
Yes.

545
00:31:49,720 --> 00:31:54,440
He pulls a gun out of his pocket, and he holds it to his own head, and he says, if you vote

546
00:31:54,440 --> 00:31:56,940
to condemn me, I will blow out my brains in front of you.

547
00:31:57,040 --> 00:31:59,820
Better than threatening to blow out the brains of Girondins, basically.

548
00:31:59,860 --> 00:32:00,240
I guess so.

549
00:32:00,660 --> 00:32:01,960
People are very impressed by this.

550
00:32:02,020 --> 00:32:04,160
They say, well, this obviously shows that Marat's a tremendous fellow.

551
00:32:04,780 --> 00:32:08,960
And the result of this is that they're not kicked out.

552
00:32:09,260 --> 00:32:11,720
So the Montagnards have made a bit of a mistake here.

553
00:32:13,120 --> 00:32:15,480
I was about to say they've wounded but not killed their opponent.

554
00:32:15,540 --> 00:32:16,920
They've barely even wounded them.

555
00:32:17,140 --> 00:32:17,460
Yeah.

556
00:32:17,500 --> 00:32:19,200
I mean, if you're going to shoot at the king, you better kill him.

557
00:32:19,200 --> 00:32:19,880
Exactly.

558
00:32:20,020 --> 00:32:24,020
So it is clear from this point that this feud is only ever going to get worse.

559
00:32:24,580 --> 00:32:26,160
And a few weeks go by.

560
00:32:26,340 --> 00:32:31,160
The Girondins basically are sort of constantly niggling at the Montagnards, but never bringing

561
00:32:31,160 --> 00:32:31,760
them down.

562
00:32:31,960 --> 00:32:36,800
Also, the Girondins, as time goes by, they're actually beginning to lose a bit of support

563
00:32:36,800 --> 00:32:40,740
from the plain, from the independent people, because the independent people actually are

564
00:32:40,740 --> 00:32:41,620
a bit sick of this.

565
00:32:41,740 --> 00:32:47,020
And also, the Girondins are quite corrupt, and they're very overbearing, and they're

566
00:32:47,020 --> 00:32:49,060
very kind of hungry for power and bossy.

567
00:32:49,060 --> 00:32:49,180
Of course.

568
00:32:49,200 --> 00:32:50,240
They go to all their dinner parties, haven't they?

569
00:32:50,240 --> 00:32:51,100
They go to their dinner parties.

570
00:32:51,120 --> 00:32:52,140
High-flying dinner parties.

571
00:32:52,460 --> 00:32:52,860
Yeah.

572
00:32:52,860 --> 00:32:53,200
Exactly.

573
00:32:53,440 --> 00:32:54,680
Inviting the normal people.

574
00:32:54,900 --> 00:32:55,100
Right.

575
00:32:55,160 --> 00:33:00,580
And if you're just a bog-standard deputy from provincial France, you're sick of being lectured

576
00:33:00,580 --> 00:33:04,800
by Brissot and having him slagging off the Montagnards and stuff, you just become very

577
00:33:04,800 --> 00:33:05,700
impatient with all this.

578
00:33:06,040 --> 00:33:07,720
And I think the Girondins know this.

579
00:33:08,000 --> 00:33:10,880
So towards the end of October, they decide to have another go.

580
00:33:10,960 --> 00:33:13,480
And this time, they will just go for Robespierre, and they'll go for him personally.

581
00:33:13,660 --> 00:33:19,040
And they get a newspaper editor called Jean-Baptiste Louvet to stand up and accuse Robespierre of

582
00:33:19,200 --> 00:33:23,080
trying to make himself dictator, of having a personality cult.

583
00:33:23,340 --> 00:33:26,220
Now, again, they reach for a Roman parallel, interestingly.

584
00:33:26,220 --> 00:33:26,740
Of course they do.

585
00:33:27,080 --> 00:33:34,200
So Louvet deliberately models his speech on Cicero unveiling the Catiline conspiracy.

586
00:33:34,620 --> 00:33:37,680
And of course, that's the analogy that everybody knows.

587
00:33:37,920 --> 00:33:38,820
They've all grown up with it.

588
00:33:38,840 --> 00:33:39,660
They've all done it at school.

589
00:33:40,080 --> 00:33:40,920
It's been on their minds.

590
00:33:40,920 --> 00:33:43,400
How long will you abuse our patience, Robespierre?

591
00:33:43,660 --> 00:33:44,520
All of that stuff.

592
00:33:44,620 --> 00:33:44,840
Exactly.

593
00:33:45,160 --> 00:33:46,480
And Louvet says, come on.

594
00:33:46,540 --> 00:33:47,400
France has a choice.

595
00:33:47,480 --> 00:33:48,240
There are only two parties.

596
00:33:48,240 --> 00:33:49,360
And France must choose.

597
00:33:49,780 --> 00:33:53,320
There is the party of us, the Girondins, we're the party of philosophers.

598
00:33:54,000 --> 00:33:57,840
And on the other hand, there is the mountain, they're the party of murderers.

599
00:33:58,060 --> 00:33:58,600
Put it like that.

600
00:33:59,040 --> 00:33:59,700
Yeah, exactly.

601
00:33:59,860 --> 00:34:03,700
If I was facing attack from the Prussians, and I had to choose one of those two parties

602
00:34:03,700 --> 00:34:06,320
to represent me, I definitely wouldn't choose the philosophers.

603
00:34:06,820 --> 00:34:08,100
But do you know who's there for this?

604
00:34:08,560 --> 00:34:09,980
Well, you do know, because I know you've got the notes.

605
00:34:10,260 --> 00:34:14,440
But also I know this, because I'm a big fan of his, is the great poet William Wordsworth.

606
00:34:14,600 --> 00:34:16,200
He's basically on his gap year, isn't he?

607
00:34:16,280 --> 00:34:16,860
Yeah, he is.

608
00:34:16,860 --> 00:34:17,760
He's on a holiday.

609
00:34:18,340 --> 00:34:19,260
Well, he's not on holiday.

610
00:34:19,360 --> 00:34:21,640
He's graduated and doesn't know what to do with himself.

611
00:34:21,880 --> 00:34:26,840
So he's gone off to France, supposedly to improve his knowledge of French.

612
00:34:27,220 --> 00:34:29,800
But actually, he's having an affair with a woman in Orléans, got her pregnant.

613
00:34:30,100 --> 00:34:31,980
But that would improve your French though, surely.

614
00:34:32,120 --> 00:34:32,640
Yes, it would.

615
00:34:32,840 --> 00:34:34,060
Give us a bit of poetry, Tom.

616
00:34:34,120 --> 00:34:35,160
I know you love a bit of Wordsworth.

617
00:34:35,320 --> 00:34:40,540
When a dead pause ensued and no one stirred, in silence of all present, from his seat Louvet

618
00:34:40,540 --> 00:34:46,020
walked single through the avenue and took his station in the tribune, saying, I, Robespierre,

619
00:34:46,020 --> 00:34:47,020
accuse thee.

620
00:34:48,620 --> 00:34:51,860
Now, that line is actually a bit confusing, isn't it?

621
00:34:51,880 --> 00:34:55,240
Because it makes it sound like Robespierre is claiming to be-

622
00:34:55,240 --> 00:34:55,780
Exactly.

623
00:34:56,000 --> 00:34:59,440
I don't want to diss Wordsworth, one of our greatest ever poets.

624
00:34:59,840 --> 00:35:02,720
Wordsworth lets himself down there with his lack of clarity.

625
00:35:03,060 --> 00:35:07,080
So this is in the prelude, which he writes much later when he's become a counter-revolutionary

626
00:35:07,080 --> 00:35:08,000
and a massive reactionary.

627
00:35:08,500 --> 00:35:09,680
Shall I carry on?

628
00:35:09,840 --> 00:35:10,020
Do.

629
00:35:10,220 --> 00:35:10,780
We'd love that.

630
00:35:10,920 --> 00:35:11,480
A bit more poetry?

631
00:35:11,660 --> 00:35:12,260
Yeah, I love it.

632
00:35:12,640 --> 00:35:13,040
Okay.

633
00:35:13,040 --> 00:35:13,540
Well is known.

634
00:35:13,540 --> 00:35:18,560
The inglorious issue of that charge and how he who had launched the startling thunderbolt.

635
00:35:18,680 --> 00:35:19,320
So that's Louvet.

636
00:35:19,540 --> 00:35:24,660
The one bold man whose voice the attack had sounded was left without a follower to discharge

637
00:35:24,660 --> 00:35:31,460
his perilous duty and retired lamenting that heaven's best aid is wasted upon men who to

638
00:35:31,460 --> 00:35:35,520
themselves are false, which is a long way of saying that Louvet's attack doesn't work

639
00:35:35,520 --> 00:35:37,800
and he doesn't get the support that he'd been expecting.

640
00:35:38,020 --> 00:35:40,180
And it's all a bit of an embarrassment and a letdown.

641
00:35:40,340 --> 00:35:41,180
It is an embarrassment.

642
00:35:41,260 --> 00:35:41,560
Exactly.

643
00:35:41,560 --> 00:35:44,360
Wordsworth in a very convoluted way is quite right.

644
00:35:44,920 --> 00:35:47,400
And actually Rob Spierre completely wins the day.

645
00:35:47,500 --> 00:35:51,420
So a week later, Rob Spierre, he buys his time and then he makes his response.

646
00:35:51,800 --> 00:35:57,960
And he says, um, I have encouraged violence, he says, but I did it because it was the only

647
00:35:57,960 --> 00:35:59,000
way to save the country.

648
00:35:59,080 --> 00:36:02,360
And he says to everybody, do you want to have a revolution without a revolution?

649
00:36:02,660 --> 00:36:06,000
I mean, we have to, you know, I did it and I was right to do it.

650
00:36:06,060 --> 00:36:10,800
Because people may remember when we, when we talked about the, um, guillotine, um, that,

651
00:36:10,800 --> 00:36:13,040
uh, Rob Spierre was against capital punishment.

652
00:36:13,360 --> 00:36:18,360
But he is now saying that the, the travails of the Republic are such that the real crime

653
00:36:18,360 --> 00:36:23,700
would not be to support the elimination of those who threaten France and the nation.

654
00:36:23,880 --> 00:36:28,680
And as we discussed in the September massacres episode, a majority clearly agree

655
00:36:28,680 --> 00:36:29,100
with that.

656
00:36:29,180 --> 00:36:32,720
They think we're at war, you know, we've always executed people.

657
00:36:32,860 --> 00:36:38,220
It's not like public executions and the public display of violence is a, is a novelty.

658
00:36:38,960 --> 00:36:40,400
It's completely reasonable.

659
00:36:40,400 --> 00:36:43,540
It's reasonable for us to use violent measures to preserve the Republic.

660
00:36:44,400 --> 00:36:46,260
And so Rob Spierre wins the day.

661
00:36:46,520 --> 00:36:48,580
He gets torrents of applause in the convention.

662
00:36:49,540 --> 00:36:53,680
Brissot and the Girondins are kind of fuming, they're sort of like sort of cartoon characters

663
00:36:53,680 --> 00:36:55,860
kind of clenching their fists with braid.

664
00:36:56,080 --> 00:36:58,580
You pesky Montagnards are kind of on the benches.

665
00:36:58,980 --> 00:37:03,200
And Brissot writes to Dumouriez and he says, this is absolutely intolerable.

666
00:37:03,300 --> 00:37:06,420
He says, I spend all my time fighting these miserable anarchists.

667
00:37:06,440 --> 00:37:07,480
That's a direct quote.

668
00:37:07,660 --> 00:37:09,160
When I should be concentrating.

669
00:37:09,160 --> 00:37:15,640
On the uprising of the entire planet, I mean, which is a bonkers thing to say, but quite

670
00:37:15,640 --> 00:37:16,480
Trotsky, isn't it?

671
00:37:16,540 --> 00:37:21,000
It's very Trotsky, but you can see why he's saying it because we're now six weeks

672
00:37:21,000 --> 00:37:25,080
after the battle of Valmy and since then, Tom, there have been some unbelievably dramatic

673
00:37:25,080 --> 00:37:26,680
scenes on the battlefield.

674
00:37:27,040 --> 00:37:32,200
As Dumouriez managed to invade Belgium, which you said was, is a long-term kind of plan.

675
00:37:32,580 --> 00:37:33,020
Excitingly.

676
00:37:33,020 --> 00:37:33,280
Yes.

677
00:37:33,660 --> 00:37:34,620
Oh, bless him.

678
00:37:34,760 --> 00:37:37,500
So for once, somebody in the story has actually got what he wanted.

679
00:37:37,780 --> 00:37:38,540
So basically.

680
00:37:39,160 --> 00:37:39,520
That's what's happened.

681
00:37:39,680 --> 00:37:41,580
I know you love military history, Tom.

682
00:37:42,600 --> 00:37:46,780
The Prussians have been hanging around outside Valmy pouring with rain after this kind of

